        "body": "Integrating three external systems comes with constraints that apply to anything of this shape. Nothing spans a CRM, a database and a payment processor as a single transaction. A subscription can be created at one vendor and the matching local write can fail a moment later, and no rollback exists that reaches across a vendor boundary. An integration like this therefore has to be reconcilable rather than merely correct on the happy path. External objects need local references pointing back at their counterparts. Inbound events need to be safe to process more than once, because webhook delivery retries, duplicates and arrives out of order by design rather than by accident. State needs to be re-derivable by reading back from the source system, so that no single missed message is unrecoverable.\n\nThe other standing constraint is organisational rather than technical. Three vendors change on their own release schedules and enforce their own rate limits, and none of them consults the platform first. Keeping each integration behind a narrow internal interface is the ordinary defence, because it decides in advance how far a change on someone else's calendar is allowed to travel. Rate limits are a design input in the same way, because a call pattern that is unremarkable for one customer becomes a queueing problem at volume."
